Sending keys: ['cat << \'EOF\' > generate_answers.py\nimport json\n\npaxlovid_id = \'8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a\'\nlop_rit_id = \'4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85\'\n\nanswers_dict = {\n    "b2531657ba5c7a78": {\n        "answer": "Paxlovid (nirmatrelvir co-packaged with ritonavir) is a strong inhibitor of CYP3A, and an inhibitor of CYP2D6, P-gp and OATP1B1. Co-administration of Paxlovid with drugs primarily metabolized by CYP3A and CYP2D6 or transported by P-gp or OATP1B1 may result in increased plasma concentrations of such drugs, increasing the risk of severe, life-threatening, or fatal events. Additionally, nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are CYP3A substrates; therefore, concomitant use of strong CYP3A inducers may decrease Paxlovid plasma concentrations and reduce its therapeutic effect.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0000",\n                "section": "Boxed Warning",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0019",\n                "section": "7.1 Potential for PAXLOVID to Affect Other Drugs",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID (nirmatrelvir co-packaged with ritonavir) is a strong inhibitor of CYP3A, and an inhibitor of CYP2D6, P-gp and OATP1B1."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0020",\n                "section": "7.2 Potential for Other Drugs to Affect PAXLOVID",\n                "supporting_quote": "Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are CYP3A substrates; therefore, drugs that induce CYP3A may decrease nirmatrelvir and ritonavir plasma concentrations and reduce PAXLOVID therapeutic effect"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    "6f12607a1ba14dd5": {\n        "answer": "Paxlovid is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions. These include: alfuzosin, ranolazine, amiodarone, dronedarone, flecainide, propafenone, quinidine, colchicine (in patients with renal and/or hepatic impairment), lurasidone, pimozide, silodosin, eplerenone, ivabradine, dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine, lovastatin, simvastatin, voclosporin, lomitapide, eletriptan, ubrogepant, finerenone, naloxegol, sildenafil (for PAH), triazolam, oral midazolam, flibanserin, and tolvaptan. Paxlovid is also contraindicated with strong CYP3A inducers that significantly reduce nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations, including: apalutamide, enzalutamide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, and St. John\'s Wort.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n                "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions and drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    "daef650b92e56473": {\n        "answer": "Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution must be taken with food. When administering the oral solution by feeding tube, polyurethane feeding tubes should not be used due to potential incompatibility with the ethanol and propylene glycol content; instead, silicone and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) feeding tubes should be used. For pediatric patients, the dose of oral solution should be administered using the supplied calibrated cup or an oral dosing syringe.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0001",\n                "section": "2.1 General Administration Recommendations",\n                "supporting_quote": "oral solution must be taken with food."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0002",\n                "section": "2.2 Administering Oral Solution by Feeding Tube",\n                "supporting_quote": "Because lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains ethanol and propylene glycol, it is not recommended for use with polyurethane feeding tubes due to potential incompatibility."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0007",\n                "section": "2.4 Dosage Recommendations in Pediatric Patients",\n                "supporting_quote": "The dose of the oral solution should be administered using the calibrated cup (supplied) or oral dosing syringe."\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    "a0576a26f2a2eeac": {\n        "answer": "The recommended dosage of lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution in adults is either 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL) once daily, or 400 mg/100 mg (5 mL) twice daily. The dose must be increased to 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL) twice daily when administered in combination with efavirenz, nevirapine, or nelfinavir. Once daily dosing is not recommended in pediatric patients, pregnant women, or in combination with carbamazepine, phenobarbital, phenytoin, efavirenz, nevirapine, or nelfinavir, or in patients with three or more lopinavir resistance-associated substitutions.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0003",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults",\n                "supporting_quote": "Lopinavir and ritonavir can be given in once daily or twice daily dosing regimen"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0004",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults",\n                "supporting_quote": "80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 800 mg/200 mg (10 mL) once daily"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0005",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults",\n                "supporting_quote": "The dose of lopinavir and ritonavir must be increased when administered in combination with efavirenz, nevirapine or nelfinavir."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0006",\n                "section": "2.3 Dosage Recommendations in Adults",\n                "supporting_quote": "80 mg/20 mg per mL Oral Solution 520 mg/130 mg (6.5 mL) twice daily"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    "9be5515bc3064979": {\n        "answer": "The safety risks of lopinavir and ritonavir include serious adverse reactions due to drug interactions (as a CYP3A inhibitor), toxicity in preterm neonates (due to ethanol and propylene glycol content), pancreatitis (which can be fatal), hepatotoxicity (especially in patients with hepatitis B or C), QT and PR interval prolongation (which can lead to atrioventricular block), diabetes mellitus/hyperglycemia, immune reconstitution syndrome, lipid elevations (large increases in cholesterol and triglycerides), fat redistribution, increased bleeding in patients with hemophilia, and resistance/cross-resistance. Commonly reported adverse reactions in adults include diarrhea (reported by at least half of patients on once-daily dosing), nausea, vomiting, hypertriglyceridemia, and hypercholesterolemia.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",\n                "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Initiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0015",\n                "section": "5.2 Toxicity in Preterm Neonates",\n                "supporting_quote": "Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains the excipients ethanol, approximately 42% (v/v) and propylene glycol, approximately 15% (w/v)."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0016",\n                "section": "5.3 Pancreatitis",\n                "supporting_quote": "Pancreatitis has been observed in patients receiving"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0028",\n                "section": "6.1 Clinical Trials Experience",\n                "supporting_quote": "reported ongoing diarrhea."\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    "18078efe9380c8dc": {\n        "answer": "Important warnings and precautions associated with lopinavir and ritonavir include: 1) Risk of serious adverse reactions due to drug interactions (as a CYP3A inhibitor); 2) Toxicity in preterm neonates due to ethanol and propylene glycol content; 3) Pancreatitis (sometimes fatal); 4) Hepatotoxicity and hepatic decompensation; 5) QT interval prolongation and torsade de pointes; 6) PR interval prolongation and second or third degree atrioventricular block; 7) New onset or exacerbation of diabetes mellitus and hyperglycemia; 8) Immune reconstitution syndrome; 9) Lipid elevations (total cholesterol and triglycerides); 10) Fat redistribution/accumulation; 11) Increased bleeding in patients with hemophilia; and 12) Potential for development of resistance and cross-resistance.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",\n                "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Initiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0015",\n                "section": "5.2 Toxicity in Preterm Neonates",\n                "supporting_quote": "Lopinavir and ritonavir oral solution contains the excipients ethanol, approximately 42% (v/v) and propylene glycol, approximately 15% (w/v)."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0016",\n                "section": "5.3 Pancreatitis",\n                "supporting_quote": "Pancreatitis has been observed in patients receiving"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0018",\n                "section": "5.5 QT Interval Prolongation",\n                "supporting_quote": "Avoid use in patients with congenital long QT syndrome"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": lop_rit_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0019",\n                "section": "5.6 PR Interval Prolongation",\n                "supporting_quote": "Cases of second or third degree atrioventricular block have been reported."\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    "fbab4114f66e2dda": {\n        "answer": "Important warnings and precautions associated with Paxlovid include: 1) Risk of serious, life-threatening, or fatal adverse reactions due to drug interactions, as ritonavir is a strong CYP3A inhibitor; 2) Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN), and Stevens-Johnson syndrome; 3) Hepatotoxicity, including transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and pancreatitis; and 4) Risk of HIV-1 resistance development in patients with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection, due to the ritonavir component.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0000",\n                "section": "Boxed Warning",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0013",\n                "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "Medications that induce CYP3A may decrease concentrations of PAXLOVID."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",\n                "section": "5.2 Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "immediately discontinue PAXLOVID and initiate appropriate medications and/or supportive care."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0015",\n                "section": "5.3 Hepatotoxicity",\n                "supporting_quote": "Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0016",\n                "section": "5.4 Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development",\n                "supporting_quote": "Because nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir"\n            }\n        ]\n    },\n    "2d63e0a82215867f": {\n        "answer": "The safety risks of Paxlovid include serious, life-threatening, or fatal adverse reactions due to drug-drug interactions, hypersensitivity reactions (including anaphylaxis, toxic epidermal necrolysis, and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), hepatotoxicity (transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, jaundice), and the risk of HIV-1 resistance development in patients with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection. In clinical trials, the most common adverse reactions (>=1% incidence and greater than placebo) were dysgeusia (5% vs <1% for placebo) and diarrhea (3% vs 2% for placebo). Other adverse reactions reported during EUA use include headache, hypertension, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and malaise.",\n        "citations": [\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0000",\n                "section": "Boxed Warning",\n                "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events"\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",\n                "section": "5.2 Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n                "supporting_quote": "immediately discontinue PAXLOVID and initiate appropriate medications and/or supportive care."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0015",\n                "section": "5.3 Hepatotoxicity",\n                "supporting_quote": "Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir."\n            },\n            {\n                "set_id": paxlovid_id,\n                "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0018",\n                "section": "6.1 Clinical Trials Experience",\n                "supporting_quote": "The most common adverse reactions (≥1% incidence in the PAXLOVID group and occurring at a greater frequency than in the placebo group) were dysgeusia (5% and <1%, respectively) and diarrhea (3% and 2%, respectively)."\n            }\n   